        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Solid deck stain is the finish for a deck that has outgrown semi-transparent: mismatched boards, grey weathering, an old stain that no longer looks right. It hides all of that in two coats. The catch is that it forms a film, and films peel when they go over damp wood, a weak surface or too many old coats. The best solid stain is the one you put on correctly, but some are genuinely better than others.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-solid-stain-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Solid Deck Stain Is, and What It Isn't
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solid deck stain, also sold as solid color or opaque stain, is a pigmented finish that hides the wood's colour and most of its grain while leaving the texture visible. It sits between semi-transparent stain, which soaks in and lets the grain show, and deck paint or resurfacer, which builds a thick film that hides texture too. Nearly every solid stain sold today is water-based acrylic, and that matters for how it behaves: it forms a thin film on the surface rather than soaking deep into the wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            That film is both the appeal and the risk. It is why a solid stain can make a mismatched, weathered or patched deck look uniform, why it lasts longer than a semi-transparent on many decks, and why it can peel when it fails. A semi-transparent wears away gradually and can be recoated after a wash; a solid stain that has lost adhesion lifts in flakes that must be scraped or stripped. If you have not yet decided between the two, our comparison of <a href="/solid-vs-semi-transparent-deck-stain/">solid vs semi-transparent deck stain</a> covers that choice in detail. This page assumes you have chosen solid and want the best one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solid stain is also not deck paint. Paint and resurfacers such as BEHR DeckOver are thicker, fill cracks and hide texture, and are the right answer for a deck too far gone for stain. A solid stain wants sound wood. If your boards are splintering, checking deeply or have half a dozen old coats on them, read our <a href="/deck-paint-vs-deck-stain/">deck paint vs deck stain</a> guide and our <a href="/best-deck-paint/">best deck paint</a> picks before buying stain.
          </p>

