        <div id="why-it-peels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Solid Stain Peels, and How to Stop It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solid stain has a reputation for peeling, and the reputation is earned. But peeling is almost never a product defect. It is the result of a film being asked to stick to something it cannot grip, or being trapped over moisture that pushes it off. Understanding the causes is the difference between a finish that lasts and one that flakes by the second summer.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first cause is moisture. Deck boards absorb water from below, from rain, and from the ground under low decks. An acrylic film breathes somewhat, but a thick one over wet wood traps vapour, and it lifts. Stain only when the wood is dry: a few drops of water should soak in within a minute or so rather than bead. New pressure-treated lumber often needs weeks or months to dry enough, which our guide on <a href="/how-long-to-wait-to-stain-a-new-deck/">how long to wait to stain a new deck</a> covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second cause is a poor surface. Solid stain over mill glaze on new boards, over grey, sun-damaged wood fibres, or over an old finish that is already failing, bonds to that weak layer, not to the wood. When the weak layer lets go, the stain goes with it. Cleaning with a deck cleaner, brightening, and stripping any loose old coating is what gives the stain something sound to hold.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third cause is film thickness. Two thin coats are stronger than one heavy coat because each dries and bonds properly. A heavy coat, or a third and fourth coat applied over years of recoating, builds a film thick enough to behave like paint, and paint on a horizontal surface peels. This is why most manufacturers specify two coats and why recoating every couple of years with a full two-coat application is a mistake. Our <a href="/deck-stain-peeling/">deck stain peeling</a> guide has the full diagnosis.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Dry wood, clean wood, thin coats.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Almost every solid stain failure traces back to one of three things: moisture in the wood, a weak surface under the stain, or a film built too thick. Get those right and any of the products on this page will last.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            One more cause is the deck's design. Low decks close to the ground, boards with no gap for drainage, and shaded decks that never dry out are hard on any film-forming finish. On those decks, a semi-transparent penetrating stain is often the better choice, and no solid stain will fix the underlying moisture problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Prep for a Solid Stain That Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prep for a solid stain is the same as for any deck finish, with one extra demand: the surface has to be sound enough for a film to grip. Start by clearing the deck, setting protruding nails, replacing rotten boards and sanding any raised splinters. Then clean the whole surface with a deck cleaner, scrubbing or using a low-pressure rinse. Oxygen bleach cleaners remove grey weathered fibres and mildew; our <a href="/best-deck-cleaner/">best deck cleaner</a> guide covers the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the deck has an existing finish, decide what to do with it. A previous semi-transparent stain that has mostly worn off can usually be cleaned and covered by a solid stain, because solid stains go over semi-transparents. A previous solid stain that is sound everywhere can be cleaned and recoated. A previous solid stain that is peeling in patches needs every loose flake removed, either by scraping and sanding or by chemical stripping. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-strip-solid-stain-from-a-deck/">how to strip solid stain from a deck</a> covers that job.
          </p>

          <p>
            After cleaning or stripping, a brightener restores the wood's pH and opens the surface. Then let the deck dry, typically 48 hours of dry weather, and test with a few drops of water. If they soak in, you are ready. If they bead, the wood is either still wet or has a residue on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the weather window on the label. Most acrylic solid stains want air and surface temperatures of at least 50°F and no rain for 24 hours; Olympic Maximum is rated down to 35°F and rain-ready in 12 hours. Avoid staining in direct midday sun, which makes the stain dry before it levels and leaves lap marks. Our guide on <a href="/what-temperature-to-stain-a-deck/">what temperature to stain a deck</a> covers the details, and <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/">how to prep a deck for staining</a> has the full checklist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Applying Solid Stain: Brush, Roller or Sprayer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solid stain is thicker than semi-transparent and goes on more like thin paint. The best results come from working two or three boards at a time along their full length, so that you are always brushing into a wet edge and never stopping in the middle of a board. Stopping mid-board leaves a lap mark that a solid colour shows more clearly than a translucent one.
          </p>

          <p>
            A good synthetic brush gives the best penetration and control, especially on the first coat, because it works the stain into the wood's surface and the end grain. A roller or pad on an extension pole is much faster on deck boards and works well if you back-brush to work the stain into gaps and texture. A sprayer is fastest of all and good for railings and balusters, but you must back-brush horizontal boards afterwards, and you need to mask the house, plants and anything else nearby. We are covering brushes and sprayers in dedicated guides: see the <a href="/best-deck-stain-brush/">best deck stain brushes</a> and <a href="/best-deck-stain-sprayer/">best deck stain sprayers</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply the first coat thin. It should look slightly uneven, not like paint. Let it dry for the recoat time on the label, which ranges from about 1 to 2 hours for DEFY to 4 to 6 hours for Cabot's acrylic, then apply a second thin coat. The second coat is what gives the full colour and the durability. On rails and fences you can sometimes get away with one coat; on deck floors, two thin coats is the standard for a reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan the order: rails and balusters first, so drips land on boards you have not stained yet, then the floor, working from the far end towards the exit. Keep a wet rag for drips on siding. Allow at least 24 hours before light foot traffic and 48 to 72 hours before furniture goes back, longer in cool or humid weather.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water-vs-oil" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Water-Based Acrylic vs Oil-Based Solid Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every solid deck stain sold today is water-based acrylic, and all six on this list are. Oil-based solid stains still exist, Cabot makes one for example, but they are harder to find in ready-mixed colours online and are restricted or reformulated in some states by VOC rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            The argument for acrylic is practical. It dries in hours rather than a day, cleans up with water, holds colour well under UV, and stays slightly flexible, which helps it move with the wood through wet and dry seasons. It also does not feed mildew the way some natural oils can. For a deck that will get a full two-coat application on a clean surface, acrylic is the better choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The argument for oil is penetration. An oil-based solid stain soaks into the surface a little more and forms a softer film, so when it fails it tends to wear rather than peel in sheets. Some contractors prefer oil for that reason on decks with a history of peeling. The trade is longer dry times, solvent cleanup and fewer colour choices.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can generally put a water-based solid stain over a previous oil-based finish once the oil has weathered and the deck has been cleaned, but not the reverse without stripping. Our full comparison of <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-deck-stain/">oil-based vs water-based deck stain</a> goes deeper, and if you are set on oil, our <a href="/best-oil-based-deck-stain/">best oil-based deck stain</a> guide is the place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="colour-and-heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Choosing a Colour, and Why Dark Decks Get Hot
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solid stain gives you a far wider colour range than semi-transparent: whites, greys, blues, greens and reds, as well as the usual browns, and custom tints at paint counters. Two practical considerations should shape the choice more than the swatch card.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first is heat. Dark solid colours absorb more sunlight and can make a deck uncomfortable or even painful underfoot on a hot afternoon. That is the problem Cabot's Heat-Reducing Solid Color Stain is built to solve, with Cabot claiming up to 20°F lower surface temperatures than other solid stains. Pale greys and tans already stay cooler. If your deck is in full sun and you want a dark colour, either pay for the heat-reducing formula or accept that shoes will be needed in July.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is wear. Every deck colour will show traffic patterns over time, but very light colours show dirt and very dark ones show the lighter wood fibres when the film wears through at the steps and door thresholds. Mid-tone browns and greys hide both best. A colour close to the natural wood also makes small worn patches less obvious between recoats.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many owners choose a solid stain for rails and a semi-transparent for the floor, or a darker solid on the floor and a lighter one on rails. Our <a href="/deck-stain-colors-guide/">deck stain colours guide</a> covers popular combinations and how each looks after a season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much Solid Stain You Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coverage on the data sheets ranges widely: 400 to 500 square feet per gallon per coat on smooth wood for Cabot's acrylic, 350 to 500 for Olympic Maximum, 200 to 300 per coat for DEFY, 150 to 400 for Thompson's, and about 250 for the one-coat Wolman. Weathered, rough or porous wood soaks up more and lands at the low end of every range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure the deck area, add the stairs and the rails (a common rule of thumb is to count a railing as about the same area as its length times its height, doubled for both sides), and divide by the low end of your stain's coverage figure. Then double it for two coats. A 12 by 20 foot deck is 240 square feet of floor; with a railing and steps, call it 400 square feet of surface, which at 250 square feet per gallon per coat needs about 3.2 gallons for two coats. Buy four.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy all the stain at once, from the same batch if possible, especially for tinted colours. Small colour differences between batches show clearly in a solid finish. Our <a href="/how-much-deck-stain-do-i-need/">deck stain calculator guide</a> has a fuller worksheet.
          </p>

          <p>
            A few brands, including BEHR's solid colour stains at Home Depot and Sherwin-Williams' SuperDeck solid colour stain at its own stores, are sold mainly through those retailers rather than online by the gallon, which is why they are not on this list. Both are solid products, and the same prep and application rules apply if you buy them. Ready Seal, often recommended for decks, does not make a solid colour stain at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining wet wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A film over damp wood traps moisture and lifts. Wait for at least 48 hours of dry weather after cleaning, and do the water-drop test: if drops bead instead of soaking in within a minute or so, the wood is not ready for a solid stain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting on one thick coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy coat looks finished faster but dries poorly and builds a film thick enough to peel like paint. Two thin coats, each allowed to dry for the label's recoat time, bond better and last longer on deck floors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating over peeling stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New stain over loose old stain only bonds to the failing layer, so it peels with it. Scrape, sand or strip every loose flake first. Over a sound solid stain, clean and recoat; over a peeling one, remove it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using solid stain to rescue a ruined deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solid stain wants sound wood. It will not fill cracks, hide splinters or hold together badly weathered boards. Replace damaged boards, or use a resurfacer or deck paint designed to bridge those defects.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the temperature range</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most acrylic solid stains want 50°F and up with no rain for a day. Applying in cold or damp conditions, or in direct midday sun, ruins the bond or leaves lap marks. Read the label window and plan the weekend around it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

