        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best solid deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most decks, Cabot's Solid Color Decking Stain, a water-based acrylic made specifically for decks, with coverage of 400 to 500 square feet per gallon on smooth wood and a 4 to 6 hour recoat window. Olympic Maximum is the better choice for cool or unreliable weather, since it is rated down to 35°F.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does solid deck stain peel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, because it forms a film. Peeling usually comes from staining damp wood, staining over a weak or failing surface, or building the film too thick with heavy coats or too many recoats. Clean, dry wood and two thin coats prevent most failures.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does solid deck stain last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a deck floor, typically several years before it needs recoating, longer on rails and fences. Thompson's, for example, is listed with a 6-year guarantee on decks and 15 years on fences and siding, which shows how much harder foot traffic and standing water are on a finish.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is solid stain the same as deck paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Solid stain is thinner and leaves the wood's texture visible, and it needs sound wood. Deck paint and resurfacers build a thicker film that hides texture and can bridge small cracks. Paint peels in larger sheets when it fails, and is harder to remove.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put solid stain over semi-transparent stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, once the old stain has weathered and the deck has been cleaned. Solid stains cover semi-transparents well. You cannot go the other way: a semi-transparent will not cover or penetrate through a solid stain, which has to be stripped first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of solid deck stain do you need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two thin coats on deck floors is the standard most manufacturers specify, with the first allowed to dry for the label's recoat time. One-coat products like Wolman DuraStain exist, but they lay down a thicker single film, which suits rails and fences better than busy floors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is oil-based or water-based solid stain better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most decks, water-based acrylic: it dries in hours, cleans up with water, holds colour well and stays flexible. Oil-based solid stains penetrate a little more and tend to wear rather than peel, but they dry slowly, need solvent cleanup and come in fewer colours.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
